     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Thursday, April 12, 2018 at 12:15:41 PM UTC-4, Zhian Kamvar wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>Technically, expected and observed heterozygosity are calculated by adegenet (unless you are referring to the &quot;Hexp&quot; column in the poppr() function). </div><div><br></div><div>Unfortunately, diversity_ci() can only work for multilocus genotypes due to the differences between the type of data represented by multilocus genotype counts and alleles. However, you can roll-your-own with the shufflepop() function (assuming that you want to get an estimate of these values in a random-mating scenario). Here are the steps:</div><div><br></div><div>1. Generate 1000 simulations of your data from allele frequencies with shufflepop(method = 2)</div><div>2. Calculate expected and observed heterozygosity for each</div><div>3. Calculate the 5% and 95% quantiles.</div><div><br></div><div>Here&#39;s an example using data from a colony of cats in Nancy, France:</div><div><br></div><div><pre><code><font face=\"Noto Mono\"><span>suppressPackageStartupMessages(library</span>(<span>&quot;poppr&quot;</span>))\r\ndata(nancycats)\r\nnan9 &lt;- nancycats[pop = <span>9</span>]\r\nhet &lt;- <span>function</span>(i){x &lt;- summary(i);c(Hexp = mean(x$Hexp), Hobs = mean(x$Hobs))}\r\nnan9samples &lt;- replicate(<span>1000</span>, shufflepop(nan9, method = <span>2</span>))\r\nres &lt;- sapply(nan9samples, het)\r\napply(res, <span>1</span>, quantile, c(<span>0.05</span>, <span>0.95</span>))\r\n<span>#&gt;          Hexp      Hobs</span>\r\n<span>#&gt; 5%  0.5781893 0.5679012</span>\r\n<span>#&gt; 95% 0.6556927 0.7407407</span>\r\nhet(nan9) <span># observed</span>\r\n<span>#&gt;      Hexp      Hobs </span>\r\n<span>#&gt; 0.6556927 0.7407407</span></font></code></pre></div><div><br></div><div>Hope that helps,</div><div>Zhian</div><div><br></div><div><div>\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">-----</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Zhian N. Kamvar, Ph. D.</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Postdoctoral Researcher (Everhart Lab)</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">Department of Plant Pathology</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">University of Nebraska-Lincoln</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\">ORCID: 0000-0003-1458-7108</div><div style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px\"><br></div><br></div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Apr 12, 2018, at 09:49 , Karl Fetter &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">karl....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br><div><